meta {
  name: Ingest Document Webhook
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

post {
  url: {{stb_worker_host}}/api/ingest
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  X-Worker-Secret: {{workerSecret}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "document_id": "1436c851-886a-4796-986a-5fab9c250433",
    "tenant_id": "9462a645-c164-4878-8171-8b35d26ace4f"
  }
}

vars:pre-request {
  stb_worker_host: https://worker.dokyudo.my.id
}

docs {
  Webhook receiver for the Supabase pg_net trigger, fired when a document status becomes 'confirmed'. Requires the X-Worker-Secret header (401 without it).

  The worker resolves the document's storage_path from Supabase (extension-aware), then:
  - .pdf: extracts text per page with PyMuPDF and chunks with tiktoken (1000 tokens, 150 overlap).
  - .docx / .doc: converts to PDF headless with LibreOffice (writer + math components — libreoffice-math is required or OMML equations vanish from the PDF), uploads the converted PDF to MinIO as {tenant}/{docId}.pdf (the frontend viewer uses this), extracts text from the DOCX itself (python-docx: paragraphs, tables, text boxes) with OMML equations converted to LaTeX wrapped in $...$ / $$...$$, chunks the text, then aligns every chunk to the converted PDF pages via rare 25-char alnum n-grams.
  - .txt: converts to PDF (LibreOffice) for the viewer, reads the original text with an encoding chain (UTF-8 BOM -> UTF-16 BOM -> UTF-8 -> cp1252), rejects binary files with NUL bytes (except UTF-16), chunks and aligns pages like DOCX.
  - .md: LibreOffice opens markdown as plain text, so it is first rendered to styled HTML (python-markdown: tables, fenced code) and that HTML is converted to PDF — the viewer shows the RENDERED markdown. Chunk text comes from the same HTML with tags stripped (no markdown syntax noise in embeddings).
  - Other extensions: marked failed.

  Returns 200 immediately; ingestion runs on the background queue thread.
}
